            <div xml:id="d335">

                <pb facs="0365" n="283" sameAs="frus:frus1911/pg283"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 838.00/602.</note>
                <head>[Untitled]</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1911-07-27"><hi rendition="#small-caps">July</hi> 27,
                        1911</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>It is persistently reported that Aux Cays, the home town of the President, has
                    revolted; the situation is becoming more serious, as other towns in the west
                    have revolted; sentiment is strongly in favor of Simon leaving the country at
                    once; most of the cabinet are of this opinion but are afraid to advise him;
                    prominent men state that Simon’s remaining complicates the situation; may
                    prevent competent respectable persons from obtaining control of the Government
                    and may lead to unnecessary bloodshed; this opinion the minister shares. He has
                    been asked to inform the President of the true conditions and to advise him to
                    leave, but has refused to do so, stating that he can take no such step. Congress
                    is hurriedly passing numerous concessions because of better terms offered, and
                    it is impossible to keep track of legislation. The consul at Port de Paix
                    reports quiet there, but thinks the committee incompetent to maintain order.</p>
            </div>
